'''
Machine Learning
--------------------
1. Supervised Learning
    - It requires labeled data. (Feature and Target Both)
    - It predicts or recommends continuous data.
    i. Regression
        - Its target value must be continuous data.
        1. Linear Regression
            a. Polynomial Regression
            b. Ridge Regression
            c. Lasso Regression
            d. Elastic Net Regression

        a. Regression Metrics
            i. MAE (Mean Absolute Error)
            ii. MSE (Mean Squared Error)
            iii. RMSE (Root Mean Squared Error / Loss Function)
            iv. R2 Score (Residual Error / Cost Function)

            - Euclidean Distance (root (x2 - x1)2 + (y2 - y1)2)

            - Loss Function Increases then Cost Function Decreases.
            - Loss Function Decreases then Cost Function Increases. (Correct)

    ii. Classification
        - Its target value must be class based (Binary classification or Multi-class classification)
        - It predicts or recommends class based data.
        1. Logistic Regression
        2. KNN (K-Nearest Neighbors)
        3. SVM (Support Vector Machine)
        4. Decision Tree and Random Forest

        a. Classification Metrics
            i. Classification Report
                - How well can our model gives accurate result?
                
            ii. Confusion Matrix
                - True Positive, False Positive, False Negative, True Negative
                - How well our model can perform?

2. Unsupervised Learning
    - It requires unlabeled data. (Features)
    i. Clustering
        a. K-Means Clustering
        b. PCA (Principle Component Analysis)

        Clustering Metrics
        ----------------------
        a. Silhouette Score
            - How well our target clusters are formed?
'''


'''
Process of Machine Learning
-----------------------------
1. EDA

2. Separate Features and Target

3. Train and Test Data
    - Rule -> 80/20 Split -> 80% Training Data and 20% Testing Data
    - 70/30, 60/40, 50/50
    - Train and Test are only for supervised learning.

4. Feature Scaling
    - To reduce biasness of data.
    - Standard Scaler
        - If a row of data is not in range.
        - (Xi - mean)/sd
        - Best Range (-1 to 1)
        - Considerable Range (3 to -3)
        - More than 3 or -3 are outliers.
        
    - Min Max Scaler
        - If a row of data is in range.
        - (Xi - min)/(max - min)
        - Range (0 to 1)

5. Define Model
    - fit -> learn training data (80% features, 80% target)
    - predict -> use 20% feature test data for further implementation

6. Metrics
    - How much model correctly predict data? (Accuracy)
    - Predict data != Actual Target Data
'''
